<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radhanites</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Radanites, Radhanite, Radanim, ar-Raðaniyya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Radhanites were early medieval Jewish merchants active in the trade between Christendom and the Muslim world from roughly the 8th to the 10th centuries. They are known chiefly from the Book of Roads and Kingdoms of Ibn Khordadbeh, written in about 870, which describes them as multilingual and traces four routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. They carried goods of small bulk and high demand, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, perfumes, jewellery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, and also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, swords and slaves. Whether the name meant a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, a clan or Jewish merchants in general is unclear. Their activity appears to cease in the 10th century, after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar</a> state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radhanite.jpg" alt="Radhanites" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Briangotts · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Radhanites2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ships from Basra and Siraf sailed to India and China, and a whole section of the market of east Baghdad sold Chinese goods, from silk to porcelain; caravans brought spices, gold and slaves, and Jewish merchants known as the <b>Radhanites</b> linked the Muslim world with Christian Europe.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li></ul></section>
